Image description: The attached figure shows a rectangular flag surface, divided into small squares of 6 columns horizontally and 4 rows vertically. The boundary of the gray "pigeon" passes through several grid points: upper-left corner A, interior point B, upper-edge point C, right-edge midpoint D, lower-edge point E, and point F near the lower left. In the boundary, AB, BC, CD, DE are circular arcs, and EF and FA are straight line segments.

Reasoning: According to the method draft, take coordinates A=(0,0), B=(2,2), C=(4,0), D=(6,2), E=(4,4), F=(1,3). First use the chords of these circular arcs to replace the circular arcs, obtaining hexagon ABCDEF. Use the shoelace formula to calculate the area:
forward sum = 0·2 + 2·0 + 4·2 + 6·4 + 4·3 + 1·0 = 44;
reverse sum = 0·2 + 2·4 + 0·6 + 2·4 + 4·1 + 3·0 = 20;
hexagon area = (44-20)/2 = 12 small-square areas.
The circular-arc correction areas produced by the four circular arcs relative to the chords are pairwise the same and one plus and one minus cancel out, so the actual "pigeon" area is still 12 small-square areas. It is known that the pigeon area is 192 cm², therefore the area of each small square is 192/12 = 16 cm², and the side length of a small square is 4 cm. The whole flag is 6 squares × 4 squares, so the dimensions are 6·4 cm × 4·4 cm = 24 cm × 16 cm.

